__TOC__
==Osmumten's Fang==
[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=254s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ayiza:''' For those who don’t know, a hotfix went out today that changes it so outside the Tombs of Amascut; it will now re-roll only the player’s accuracy and not the monster’s defence during combat calculations.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' However, whilst you’re inside the Tombs, the previous behaviour, which allowed both the player’s accuracy and the monster’s defence to reroll, will apply. So it essentially works as it did before the change whilst you’re inside Tombs of Amascut. Outside, it has had a slight change which has effectively reduced its overall DPS as it is less accurate than before.

===Question #1===
{{Cquote2|Players have pointed out the Fang accuracy calculation was never "bugged", and it worked exactly the way it was polled. Why did we change it now?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=302s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Kieren:''' There are two separate issues here. One: Communication and confusion. Second: How powerful should the Fang be? As much as we talked about what we intended to do, there may have been confusion internally and confusion in what we said to the community.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' There have been a lot of versions of the Fang, from the original blogs to what it ended up being. As much as we might have messed up in terms of communication, what we’ve said and how it has been interpreted, we should try and set the record straight, but the Fang cannot be left as strong as it was.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' At the end of the day, I believe there are two perspectives. If you’re on a main account, the item was sitting not far over 100 mil. It’s not intended to be the Scythe or the T-Bow of ToA. That position is taken by the Shadow, and that’s where we want the Shadow to be. We want the Fang to be good - not incredible. It shouldn’t be useless, and its only use shouldn’t be ToA.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I realise that’s ambiguous, but we want it to have some utility. In fact, what we said in the blog was that we expected it to have a few places, but not many. We want to open up space where we can start adding monsters that have higher defence, and this would be a use for the Fang in future.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Ultimately with the changes we’ve made, if we feel that it hasn’t met that criteria and hasn’t got decent utility across the game where it’s the go-to weapon, we can make more changes. However, we cannot let an item that’s 150 mil and only going down at the time be a general-purpose best-in-slot weapon. It was out-DPSing - or competing with, at the very least - the Dragon Hunter Lance on Dragons! We can’t have it sit at that price range and that rarity and do that.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' In all the cases where the Scythe was better than it, or other weapons were better than it, it was a small enough jump that players wouldn’t spend a billion on an item that was only marginally better than the Fang.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Looking at this from the perspective of Ironmen, it just broke the journey of the account. Suddenly there was less need to go and get a rapier. There was less need to do all these other bits of content. You could just go to ToA straight away; get your Fang, and you’re basically set for 90% of the content! That couldn’t be left as it was.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I realise maybe 90% is an over-exaggeration, but we had to address it, and we had to be strict because we care about the future of the game; the future of PvM content that we’re going to launch and the future of reward space for future PvM content as well. We need to get it in the right spot. 
* '''Mod Kieren:''' A lot of comments that I’ve seen - that the team has seen - have been to ‘make it rarer’; to make it suitably rare. If it was the same rarity as the Shadow, maybe it would have a price that warrants its power. That ship sailed, which is the problem with this. It’s already live and has been pumped into the game and the economy at the rate it has been at. A lot of players have already earned it at its current rarity. It would be a big middle finger to those players who haven’t gotten it already, to the Ironmen who have missed out on getting it at the common rate, or to main accounts who missed out on getting it at a lower price.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We can marginally change drop rates, and we are open to doing that, but a drop rate nerf where we make it four or five times rarer is quite extreme, and we can’t do that lightly. Right now, we feel we’ve made the right call to try and set the Fang on a better path where it’s not so universally strong, but it does have utility.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We expected it would be one of the lower-end items in ToA. That’s how it’s been positioned in its rarity, but it ended up being way more powerful than we thought it was. We made an assumption about the way it was implemented, matching our intent, and it didn’t.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' The DPS was way better than we thought it was going to be, from confusion internally, and as seen via the latest blog as well with what we said about double accuracy versus double defence ruling. So that’s our stance for now.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Obviously, we’re not turning a blind eye and saying, ‘that’s it, shut up shop, we’re done, we’re never ever going to contemplate a tweak again’. If it isn’t good enough in what we’ve outlaid - not amazing, but not good - then we can look and see what else we can do. But we’ve got to take a tough stance on this. I realise it’s not good news. Nerfs are never good news!
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' They’re not, but believe me; it’s better to get them out of the way earlier than it is to leave them ongoing for a long time. I’ll remind everyone of the Blowpipe and how difficult of a pill that was to swallow. It had been embedded in for years, so I think it makes sense.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I saw a lot of people in chat commenting on nerfing the rarity, and I think the start of what was said was glossed over a little. The reason that the Fang is sat at the same as something like the Lightbearer, is because it was never meant to be a super rare, super strong endgame item. It was meant to be a niche filler that had a few uses - as it said in the blog.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That’s why it was originally set to be a relatively common item so that its price would then reflect its commonness. Obviously, the power side of it, as Kieren explained, was a little too off.

===Question #2===
{{Cquote2|Why was this the fix that was chosen over adjusting NPCs within the Tombs or reworking the special effect?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=729s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Kieren:''' With changing monsters, maybe you could fix it in Tombs of Amascut, but it’s much wider than this. I mentioned Dragons as an example. Do you retcon Dragons and change their stats? I’d rather not! I think that’s the wrong approach; it would change up everything in the entire game for other equipment.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' What we have done is that we have left it to be as powerful as it was in ToA. We did that with the Shadow as well - it’s cool that the item can be good at the content itself. It’s not ideal - we’d prefer the functionality to be the same across the entire game, but that’s the situation we’re in.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I think it would be wrong to change the spirit of the item. Doing a double accuracy roll is in the same spirit as what we were originally thinking with the Fang. It is quite different, and it’s quite a unique behaviour - we have nothing else that acts like that.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' You saw the original Fang pitch - it was to have a really high accuracy stat, but that’s not as interesting. It doesn’t give you a different DPS curve. We wanted to create something different, which meant that the Fang could excel in different situations. Hopefully, that’s where it now rests. To reiterate, we don’t want it to be the be-all and end-all weapon. We want it to be a good weapon. It has a few places where it’s really strong. We expect with future content; we will add more places that it’s good for as well.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Like we’ve said, it’s never an easy thing to have to consider, but I think in time, it will hopefully all make sense, especially as we now have the option for high defence monsters for it to excel in and a little bit further down the line.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We do appreciate that this isn’t the best of reasoning right now because you’ll have to wait for something to come out in the future, but at least it will remain powerful and useable within Tombs of Amascut, which will provide somewhat of a niche that we’ve mentioned originally in the first blog.

==Polling Discussion==
[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=254s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ayiza:''' First of all, we’re looking at basically giving more power to the players. The way that we’re looking to do that is by changing our approach to Polling. The current approach looks at us having really fleshed-out design documents with almost all of the details included from the very offset. That means that we have to spend a significant amount of time developing, ideating, and working through to get to that position.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' At this point, we still don’t even know whether or not it’s content that players want. We have a pretty good idea because I feel we’re okay at staying in touch and grasping the kind of things players want through various forms. But ultimately, it’s risky. We could spend two to three months working on something to put it to the Polls, and it just fails. Also, even when the content does pass it can also lead to us not having enough time to incorporate your feedback correctly.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Imagine that we spent three months developing something that’s now due to release in three months, and you want a particular aspect of this content to change entirely. That would mean we’d potentially have to go back to the drawing board and completely redesign it again, and we just don’t have time. So we have to dismiss your feedback, cut corners, or only take on the smallest changes possible to hit deadlines and stick to a roadmap that we can deliver and communicate properly to you.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' This new approach is that instead of doing fully fleshed out designs, the first step is actually to Poll a concept or an idea. We essentially say ‘do you want us to look into this, or do you want this to be added to the game? Yes or no’. If it’s a yes, then great! We know that we’re safe enough to at least start looking at developing the underlying mechanics, features and systems that make something possible.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' So if we use Tombs of Amascut as an example, this would be the base functionality to make the raid functional. The party system, the invocation system, the bosses and the mechanics - the stuff we wouldn’t normally Poll.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Once we’re in a comfortable place, we can move on to bring you, the community, on board sooner in the process, in a more detailed and in-depth way than we ever did before. We would consult on what extra features, what extra bits, and what decisions we could make that simultaneously incorporate your needs and wants.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' This may be in the form of rewards or experience rates, but it could also be specific mechanics or features. And because we’re able to do this much earlier, we can hopefully have a lot more time to be able to say, ‘that’s pretty good feedback, we can change the design now’, whereas before, we couldn’t.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That’s the first one. It’s all about giving more power to you as the players, but also giving us the ability to Poll content without having fully fleshed-out design documents so that we can get an idea of where you want us to spend the time, rather than us dictating where we’re spending the time!
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Following on from there, we’re looking at introducing a fixed Polling Charter. This one is simply just a case of us clearly defining what is and isn’t Polled going forward. That’s in effect as of last week, and it covers all of the different areas of the game that you can imagine. In essence, all of the big, impactful changes we'll still Poll, we’ll still consult, and we’ll still look to get feedback on them. But you’ll at least expect to know that small quality of life or trivial fixes, integrity balancing changes - things like that wouldn’t be Polled, and we’re just going to be able to do them.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' This should open up a lot more freedom for the team to be able to make smaller changes. We should hopefully be able to get away from situations - especially when it comes to our typical Q&A streams - where you ask us a bunch of questions, and we say that we’d love to do them, but we need to find time in the schedule and find time in the Poll systems because we had to actually Poll them.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Instead, we can add them to the backlog, and a developer can freely know they can pick that up to work on it. It’s then released the week, two weeks, three weeks afterwards, whenever it may be, but it doesn’t have to fit into a Polling schedule anymore, it can just be done. Maybe we’ll see less of the ‘we’ll put it on the backlog and consider it’ responses.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' The last change is reducing the percentage of yes votes that are required for content to pass. We’re going from 75% to 70% for all content going forward within the Poll system. The reasoning behind this is that less than 6% of our active members vote, and as we move into an idea and concept style for the initial vote, it’s obviously a lot less information. There’s a little bit more risk that’s brought forward as part of that.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We also want to make sure that the Poll system fairly represents everyone. So the stats we got in there was that less than 1.5% of people could make content fail. This adds just a little more of a buffer because we know that going down this road does offer a little bit more risk because you don’t have as much information to begin with as we would have previously provided.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We want to make sure that if you are getting a concept, there’s more chance of it getting into the game. That’s the honest truth! So we’ll just see how that goes.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' There is also quite a lot of content that narrowly missed the 75% mark that a lot of people wanted. When that happens, thousands of people who voted yes to something are now upset because their content didn’t make it into the game. We didn’t want to go too far or too drastic on this change because we’re aware that the majority vote is something players are very accustomed to, and looking at through the Annual Survey showcases that.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Regretfully we haven’t been able to fully process the Annual Survey results because it only closed on Sunday. We’re still waiting for the full results to settle down and go through, but I can safely say that we’ll look to share this in either the Gazette or we’ll do a specific stream to go over the Annual Survey results in its own right.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Around 60% of people favoured reducing it by a small amount or saying it was just about right. So the consensus is they were in favour of either keeping it how it was or reducing it by a small amount. Reducing by a small amount is what we opted to go for before we even saw the Annual Survey results, and we’re glad to have seen that coming through from the players as well. So look forward to the future stream, where we thoroughly review the Annual Survey results.

===Question #1===
{{Cquote2|What do you think of these changes? In particular, how much does the new approach with More Player Power impact the dev team?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=1350s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Kieren:''' I think it’ll be a big help! I think the biggest factor is the misnomer that we don’t dev anything until the Poll was passed. In reality, the way we were Polling content is ‘here’s Tombs of Amascut! Do you want the raid? Do you want this aspect? Do you want this reward?’ all the details would be fleshed out in advance. The problem is, fleshing out all those details takes time. That takes work.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' You’re designing every aspect of XYZ. With Guardians of the Rift, with Giants’ Foundry, we’re doing all the work to calculate XP rates and try and get an understanding of how we can do those. It’s quite difficult to really get all these details together without spending weeks and weeks of designing, reviewing, prototyping and starting to put things in place so you can get a grasp of where it’s going to sit so that we can communicate it in the detail that players have come to expect in the current Polling process.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Shifting to the new one where it would be Polling concepts and directional elements are left to later will allow us to Poll before we spend significant time on an idea. I do believe that’s what our players already largely thought was happening! But you can’t do that. It would be impossible for us to Poll all the aspects of a project without spending a lot of time on it. I think this will be really helpful for our approach and will save the dev team time - save wasted time.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' The directional elements will put a lot more power in the player's hands instead of us trying to second guess what direction players would want us to take for pieces of content. We now have an official process via this Polling booth to get input from players, and that will be so valuable.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Before we’ve already made a decision, before we’ve maybe even developed and prototyped it, having that say will help forge the content in a way that will be best for everybody. It’ll be best for the player base and the devs because we’re getting something that, ideally, players will like the first time.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I’m glad that at least it definitely comes across as a positive to the dev team! I know that many in the team are looking forward to having a little bit more creative freedom now, especially when it comes to those initial pitches and keeping a little bit of the mystery locked behind them.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We have our first Poll blog going out in the next few days for some new content, which is following the new system a little bit. Keep in mind this is the first time we’ll be using the new system, and we did already have designs in mind, at least for what we wanted to offer with this poll. It’s going to take us a bit of time to adjust to the new process ourselves internally, but I think when it comes to next year’s roadmap with all of the new content that we’ve not yet spent time on, that’s when players will start to notice the biggest difference.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I’m keen to see how the next Poll that we have going out goes down with the players because it’s very different. Thankfully, it’s some exciting side-content that everyone enjoys, so I don’t feel like we'll have any issues with it.

===Question #2===
{{Cquote2|What does the future of Restricted voting look like?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=1615s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ayiza:''' In the blog, we mentioned that we know that not everyone likes restricted voting. It definitely has its uses, and we’ve seen some really good use cases of it helping to champion change for smaller and niche communities when it’s been needed the most.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' For example, Poll 77 with Ironman-only specific questions, and we’ve had UIM-only specific questions in the past as well, because realistically - especially with something like UIM, that community is so small that there is zero chance for them to ever have content make it into the game that is specific for them without being influenced by people who will never touch the game mode. So restricted voting allows us to pinpoint those in particular.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' But at the same time, we’ve seen cases where it doesn’t, and people can get annoyed and frustrated by them. It can create friction between communities, especially with things like PvP where we want to support the PvP community and give them new content. At the same time, players feel like they should also have a say in that because even if they might not engage in the PK side of it, they are engaging in the PvP aspect when they enter these zones.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We’ve also seen how we can get PvP content to pass through a Poll without the need for restricted voting with the Wilderness Boss rework rewards.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' As you just said, it works so well for Ultimate Ironmen or Ironmen communities where it just doesn’t impact anybody else. But to go more general, I think it works well when it’s been completely unambiguous, in that there’s no debating who is an Ironman. That’s an official status; it’s quite an easy criterion. Everyone understands what it is, and the changes genuinely don’t impact anybody else.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I think it’s for similar reasons that we’ve struggled more when doing PvP-restricted votes because it has been more ambiguous about what the rule should be. Who gets to say what should happen to the Wilderness? I think it’s very legit to say that it’s not just PvPers - let alone defining who is a PvPer. Is it somebody who got one kill last year? Is it somebody who’s got 10 kills in the last month in the Wilderness? That’s ambiguous.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Then there are the players who want to do PvP but haven’t been playing it because it’s a lot less active than it used to be. Some people also use the Wilderness. If you go and train Prayer in the Wildy or do Clues in the Wildy, technically, you’re using the Wilderness as well.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' It’s much harder to define who should vote on something in a way where you’re not going to be riling up players, as we’ve seen. We’ve tried this, and the reason we’ve tried it is that we want to find a solution that helps us better go through the process of developing and following players on PvP content. But it certainly seems harder on that side.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' It’s very clear for things like UIM, Ironmen, or even very niche cases I imagine that we could find uses for with other content. If we want to look at a specific raid mechanic change, you have to have over a certain kill count to be able to vote in it because it was so particular that we don’t want it to be open to everyone.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' With something like PvP, it is difficult to have that cohort and properly identify them without alienating at least someone. That’s when I’ve personally not been a fan of restricted voting. Many years back it used to get brought up all the time by the PvP community, and I was very dead set against it because of that reason. It’s so hard to be able to turn around and say whether or not it impacts someone in a certain way; whether or not someone should have a vote as a result of that.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I’d like to think that in the future, we might be able to get a little bit closer to that with specific content. Let’s just say, for example, the PvP Arena. We know that it has no rewards right now, so very few people are engaging with it, which is okay - it was intended to be a replacement for content that was removed, rather than a flagpole piece of content in its own right. But theoretically, we could look at rewards that were only available to be used in the PvP Arena, and then that way, only people who have actually played the PvP Arena would have a say on it.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I feel that’s reasonable because the rewards have no valuable use outside of that area. That way, only those who are interested in doing it or have tried it would have a weighted vote. Maybe not for the first few rewards, just because we might not have that many people actually voting. But that could be an example, and maybe there could be other areas of PvP that we could have similar restrictions to, where items are only usable in a certain area which is very much an opt-in-only kind of situation, like Last Man Standing, for example. We could restrict that based on how much Last Man Standing participation you had.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' But as a whole, that’s where my issue with restrictive voting stands. It’s when you are trying to properly identify cohorts and restrictions and eligibility to be able to vote, it becomes very difficult. Thankfully, as shown with the Wilderness Boss rework, it’s not always needed. As long as we get decent rewards that make sense and that people are generally happy with, we can get stuff to pass.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We’ll just have to see! We’re really keen to hear more about what players feel restricted voting should look like. That’s why we didn’t look to make any changes.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' One thing that I think is worth clarifying, though, is that restricted voting still exists, and we’ll probably still continue to use it - we will just be very open and communicate clearly as and when we’re doing them, purely because as we’ve showcased, they do have really good uses at the moment, especially when it comes to the Ironman and UIM questions. Maybe there’ll be some PvP-related ones in the future that would also work, but at the moment, there’s no content we need to worry about for that just yet.

===Question #3===
{{Cquote2|I’ve seen a few comments where players are concerned the Polling Charter is just an excuse to add in content without being polled under the guise of “Game integrity updates”. Is this true?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=2040s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Kieren:''' This isn’t true. It’s not the goal here. The goal, in terms of the Charter, is to make it clear to the community what we Poll and don’t! We’re making tweaks at the same time, but previously it was so ambiguous what gets Polled and what doesn’t - we had this notion of game integrity, and that’s the only reason we didn’t Poll things.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We’ve never said explicitly ‘this is what we Poll, this is what we don’t Poll’. I think the biggest, valuable thing out of this is that the community can point to the Chart and say ‘hey, you said you’d normally Poll this sort of thing and you didn’t’, or vice-versa. That’s a good thing to have.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' It’s a good thing for the players and it’s a good thing for us because we spent how much time in the last several years in chats talking about ‘do we need to Poll this?’ when debating a quality of life change. This should help speed it up for us as well, so I think it should be good for everybody to have a Charter because it’s explicit; it’s out there, and it’s public - it’ll be on the website! You can go and read it anytime you want.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That’s a good point. I don’t think I mentioned it in the blog - it was mostly in comments - but we are getting the Polling Charter, or a form of it, uploaded to the Old School website, where it will remain as a permanent piece of content outside of the blog. You’ll be able to check it at a glance. We just wanted to make sure there was enough time to address any feedback that players may have had if we had to make changes.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' There’s one thing I want to clarify, in terms of this question in particular, because I have also seen this, and I think that players are right in pointing it out because it may come across as quite contradictory to the fact that we have recently added or looked to add or change content under the guise of game integrity without a Poll.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' This was before we even announced the Polling Charter, and the example that I’ll give is the Wilderness Boss rework. That’s something we decided to do without a Poll. Stuff like that would likely continue to happen if we felt it was necessary. For the Wilderness Boss rework, if you’ve not seen the blog already, I highly recommend checking it out. The team did a fantastic job of putting everything together for that!
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That was essentially looking at the health of the game and what the content was offering at the level of quality we’re at now. Those two were so severely mismatched that we needed to step in. We knew players had been unhappy. We’ve said it’s something that we’d do for around four or five years so, and it just never happened. That was something we did lead the charge on and looked to change without a Poll.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' In the Polling Charter, we do specifically mention that we will look to Poll changes to existing content that will have a significant impact on players. An example we have on there is reworks to existing content in areas. We used the Isle of Souls, and the Shades of Morton reworks because they offered a significant amount of new content.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Obviously, with the Wilderness Boss rework, we Polled all of the rewards, because that was quite a significant change in terms of what’s coming into the game. But outside of that, it was mostly just about making the content way better than these really weirdly abused, safe-spotted encounters that just were never intended to have been done that way, but they had to be because they were so strangely implemented. I just wanted to clarify that part.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Hopefully, as you’ve seen with the Wilderness Boss rework, we’re very open and honest when we do that. We’re not trying to shy away or force things in without you realising. We know that you’re going to know, there’s no point! We just want to say it for how it is. That’s what we’ve done, and that’s what we’ll continue to do, so if there are instances where it feels like that line has been crossed too much, then just let us know.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That’s why we have these channels open. That’s why we’re very much in the mindset of talking to you, giving opportunities for feedback, and making changes based on the things that you are and aren’t happy about - within reason, of course.

===Question #4===
{{Cquote2|Is there potential for revisiting past polls that failed by a small margin?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=2337s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Obviously, this is a response to the fact that we’re going from a 75 to 70% pass rate. I think there's 20 or 30 poll questions that have failed in that time.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' I don't see why not. We've repolled things before; this may be a better excuse to do so and see what the community thinks. There are many things people jump at, and they have their favourite issues that over the years just missed out. I look back on the reordering and resizing of the Prayer buttons and scale icons. At the time, I never got a grasp of why people did not want to be able to reorder the Prayers however they wanted. But this is the joy of the Poll process. We can revisit some of these things. I realised the Spec Orb that narrowly met failed on a recent poll. Is it right to repoll something that just failed like a few weeks ago? I feel that I’m ‘um-ing and ah-ing’ on that.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That Poll closed the day that we released the Polling Charter. So I sat there thinking, ‘this is going to look so awkward when three hours after the Poll closes, my blog is going out, and we're talking about reducing the pass rate!’. 
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' But we stayed true to the whole Polling as it was then, and it did fail. So we would need to repoll it. I think it would make absolute sense to repoll it, and I do not doubt in my mind that it would pass going forward. For that one, in particular, I saw comments of people feeling like it had failed because it would make PvP easier because they will be able to spec faster than they can currently and that's just not the case. It's just a bit of misinformation being spread around. 
* '''Mod Kieren:''' That specific change is a prime example of something we would have needed help to decide on. Should it be Polled, or should it not be Polled? Because I could very easily make an argument to say we shouldn't be Polling that; we should just change that spec or behaviour because that makes it consistent.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' It makes the game easier to understand its integrity. The problem is that we’ve never had the Charter in place, and we never had these clear, realistic rules to follow.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' The point is, it was confusing before. Now we do have that list of things to look at and say ‘okay, it fits in that category!’.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Exactly. Maybe the next Poll, maybe Poll 78, can be a revisit of past Polls that failed by a small margin. There's enoughcontent to warrant it, or we can do it in addition.

===Question #5===
{{Cquote2|Will designs be Polled before they are added to the game? And if not, what process will you take regarding community feedback on designs?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=2564s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Yes, designs will be Polled. New content designs will be polled before they’re added to the game. We will look to share a content design with you in the form of an idea or a concept pitch, very top-level, and they won't be as fully fleshed out to begin with.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That’s how we will look at things going forward. The ‘if not’ doesn’t have any relevance, because it would be ‘if’, but let’s just say, if not, what process will we take regarding community feedback on designs?
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' If you look at the Polling Changes blog, have a look at the community consultation/refinement stage. That is essentially how we'll be looking at taking on community feedback with designs. We'll put something out to you, we'll ask whether or not you want it, and we will then start working on it.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We will then come back to you and ask ‘Hey, we've started working on this. We know roughly now how difficult the content is going to be; how accessible it will be, and where it will sit. We now have a good enough idea. Here's a bit more fleshed-out design. What else do you want? What rewards do you want to add to it? Do you think this thing should work this way? Or should it actually work this way? Or do you have an entirely different suggestion that fits the current design?’.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Then we'll Poll it, and if it passes, we'll develop it and release it. In its most simple form, that's basically how it works. 

===Question #6===
{{Cquote2|Following the previous question, just in case we got it wrong. Let’s take a moment to talk about visual changes, as that was discussed a fair amount over the last few days. What will these changes mean for new rewards, changes to old areas and such?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=2664s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Kieren:''' So what I believe are changes to old equipment or old areas - anything that's sort of iconic would fall under one of our categories. If we were gonna go and update the Rune Platebody -  which I'd be very surprised if we ever wanted to - but it's so iconic! It’d totally be wrong to do that without Polling. Of course we would Poll that. The approach would be different for new stuff, though.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' For new stuff, imagine that we do everything that we've been doing recently - it's just that the item making its way into the game is dependent on whether or not that item passed the Poll in the first place, not whether or not its appearance passed the Poll.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We know that art and design are very subjective, and sometimes people have some really good feedback on how things should be changed, how things should look, or how things could fit better into the game. There have been a lot of examples where following that process has led to a really, really cool design of an item that the entire community has been really happy with. 
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' It doesn't always work out that way! Sometimes it's 50/50, and sometimes it's like a 60/40 split. For the most part, the feedback that we get from you players, especially when it comes to art and design, is super valuable. We're not looking to change that at all. When we're at the point of having designs and concepts, we'll share concept art with you as early as we can, just like we've been doing in all of our recent blogs over the last 10 months. This whole year has really stepped it up with sharing designs early on.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We’ll look to incorporate feedback as much as we can, and then whenever we do make feedback changes; we'll also share those with you and get your feedback on them. Let’s use Torva as an example with the new system. Say everyone unanimously hated the Torva design that we put out, but it's passed its poll, and we know that Torva is going to be added to the game.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' So we look at redesigning, and the conversation around it is so big that it warrants us to focus solely on that as a specific topic. We can just spin up a new blog that does not touch on the design of the actual content it's coming from, that is literally talking about the design of the armour and offering opportunities for players to give feedback through surveys and specific comments on threads that are dedicated to this particular issue. 
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Then we continue iterating until we're at a point where one of the two things happens: We feel like we're in a good enough place where the majority of the community agrees that the item itself is in a good state and that players will be happy with it released into the game as it's being offered, or we go back and repeat the process until we get to a point where we feel like it's acceptable and in a place that everyone likes. 
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We've shown that with multiple different things. I know that recently with the Voidwaker, we offered two different concepts in a blog, and they were disliked. So we went to the community, we looked at what designs they were putting out, and then we made a third one, and yes, in this case, we did Poll the three options.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That may still happen, in all honesty! It may still continue that way. We might feel we don't know because there's too much feedback on multiple designs. So we'll let the community decide. We can put that to a multiple-vote Poll, but the results of it aren't ever going to dictate whether or not it gets added to the game. That's the change. We won't be stopping items from being added because of their design.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Hopefully, that makes sense! TLDR is that we're still going to work with you, we're still going to get feedback, and we're still going to want to make sure that everything feels Old School so that we don't lose that charm.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Ultimately, the items will eventually be added to the game because being added is dictated by the first question as to whether or not we should add them. It’s not ‘should we add them based on them looking like this?’. It was more just to provide clarity rather than to say that we're taking away anything because we have also in the past been a little bit inconsistent with our approach on item designs too - where some things have gone into those multiple choice polls, some things haven't. Some items just got added without us even sharing concept art.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' So the Polling Charter there is to help explain the process. It does go into detail that we will share designs, we will get feedback and we will iterate. 

===Question #7===
{{Cquote2|Will ideas that are very popular within the community - for example, player suggestions on Reddit - be Polled for inclusion in the future?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=2985s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Kieren:''' ‘It depends on the idea’, is the ultimate answer to the question. You can look at an idea, and where does it rank on this Polling Charter? Does it meet one of those categories? Well, then yeah, it probably should be Polled. 
* '''Mod Kieren:''' In general, we want to take popular ideas. If something that’s popular in the community, of course we’ll want to put it into the game!
* '''Mod Kieren:''' It might be the case that with these changes, fewer of those need polling based on the rules. Some of them will almost certainly still need Polling, but what we set out to say is, it’s player power, right? We want to be player-driven!
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We want to be more player-driven than we've been able to do because of the Polling process. There's nothing I love more than being able to look at a popular idea - it's not just Reddit, as much as people might see Reddit and think ‘oh gosh, they never say anything smart’ - all social media, wherever it's coming from, we will look at the value of the idea.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We'll take that on board if it's a good idea. We haven’t done it for a long time, but I’d love to do more of the user-generated competitions for content, like Rooftop Agility and Motherload Mine. These are player-driven updates at their very core. We've done bits of that, but never quite to that extent. I love doing this, so I’m all for this kind of thing going forward; it's not going to really change in that sense.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We know that we want to do more Summits going forward, where we announce what content is coming out for the next few months. Let's say we follow this Summit up with a Giga Poll - a Mega Poll that has everything that we announced in the Summit.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Then we know safely that the next three, four or five months of content is locked in. Do you think that would open up a little bit more space and accessibility in the roadmap to fill in those gaps so that we could look at the community content, which we've maybe not been able to do in the past because it has been so fluid and unknown as a consequence of how long it takes us to actually Poll content?
* '''Mod Kieren:''' 100%! The idea of having the Polls locked in earlier will make it so much more possible. If something has failed, there's an obvious gap, and then the community can be involved in filling that gap. 
* '''Mod Kieren:''' On a smaller level, if we've poll a concept like ‘hey, here is our new epic boss!’ and all we've said about it is a new team boss. We don't really know even where in the world it might be at that point, perhaps. That presents a really obvious timeframe for players to be able to respond with their favourite boss ideas.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Wherever would you like to see a boss added? Would you like one in the middle of Karamja Jungle? That area may be lacking. Would you like a new vampire boss? It opens up space for those questions because it would only sometimes have all been entirely predetermined by us, with all its rewards, designs, and every other asset worked out. It's more space for more suggestions from players.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That's a really, really good point. That whole community consultation and refinement will look so different for so many different pieces of content. As I said before, we're going to have to get used to it, the players will have to get used to it, but I can fully see a future where we do just say ‘Do you want us to release a new team-based boss?’. It's then the next Poll that you get is asking 20 or 30 questions on what makes that boss the boss, and you will get to input and decide on that. I think it's really cool.

===Question #8===
{{Cquote2|Are there any plans to increase the voting requirements?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=3277s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Kieren:''' Not that I know of. Largely, the requirements are there to stop people cheating and botting Polls. You can't just see a poll and think ‘I really want a say on that’, then make an account, get the requirements in the time the poll is live, and then mass vote on it.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' So that's the basic requirement there; we're not planning to change them. If we have any reason to add a feature, obviously, it's not set in stone. These are things that can be discussed.

===Question #9===
{{Cquote2|Will there ever be a poll that asks ‘’What kind of content do you want to see’’ or “How do you feel about this” rather than a straight Yes/No option? Another popular suggestion from the community was to have questions that offered “Yes/No to proposed content/change” and “Yes/No to proposed execution”.}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=3318s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ayiza:''' The best way to answer this one is that we're going to be more experimental with the Poll system than we have been before, over the course of the next few months, or however long it may be. We tried it a little bit with the Wilderness Boss Rework. There was a question in there where we offered player input on how we should approach the change to the Revenant weapon drop rate.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That was a multiple-choice question, which we've never had in the Poll system before! It was exciting when Ash told us that it was possible and we noticed we had never really thought about doing it. I know it sounds so simple and minor, but it was a pretty big deal for us. 
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That kind of opened our eyes to ‘you know what, now that we've got that community consultation phase as part of the content approach, what can we start doing differently that we weren't able to do before?’. Before, it was just a case of, we need to know whether the content is going to pass because otherwise, it's not going to come out in time, or we've just wasted three or four months' worth of development time. 
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I imagine there'll be a lot more open-ended feedback and consultation-like questions in the Poll system and in surveys.

===Question #10===
{{Cquote2|Are there any plans to increase the 6% voting rate, perhaps by adding an incentive to voting or making polling more accessible?}}

[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Z2ky_xiRJnM&t=3415s Youtube Time Stamp]

* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I saw one example of someone suggesting that there should be a 1/5000 chance after you’ve submitted all your votes in a Poll to get a Poll Booth pet. 
* '''Mod Kieren:''' You were saying about ‘Giga Polls’. Imagine we do this big Summit stream, and we announce another 5 epic updates. Straight on the back of that, a week later, the Poll’s in-game; Giga Poll! By doing it this way, here are 5 questions on the 5 concepts, that Poll has so much more excitement going on.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' It’s not this first slog of 30 questions, answering every detail about every little thing. It’s a really important Poll and stands out from the rest because it directly decides the projects we will be doing. 
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Other polls will likely get less attention. You'll still be getting our usual QoL Polls, and you’ll get those more directional Polls that naturally fewer people will be interested in voting on. The big polls where you get a massive say in what’s to come, I think that itself could in theory change the community’s view on participation. There’s more reason to participate because this poll matters.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' Secondly, I don’t hate the idea of rewards for voting. Even if it’s something simple like a sticker you get in real life when you vote ‘’I have voted’’! A flag or a sticker that says ‘I have voted’. Just simple, fun, gimmicky cosmetics that will add a little bit of fun incentive are good things to do. I don’t have anything against them.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' But we don’t want to manipulate Polling results. We don’t want to encourage people to vote who genuinely don’t want to and artificially change the way players interact! It’s got to be a small cosmetic perk. I don’t want to add a perk that indicates ‘I voted in 100 polls in a row’, because then you’re just going to go and vote without caring about the poll. You’re gonna randomly vote and say ‘I just want my reward’. It really does need to be small.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' That’s always been my reservation in trying to incentivise people into voting. It would certainly take away the voice of the player in favour of doing it just for the reward and not actually being a true reflection of what players want.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I also saw some pretty good comments from players when I raised that point specifically on how we might combat that. We could look at everyone's default vote as ‘I don't care’ or ‘Skip question’. So if you are doing it just for a cosmetic, you can just vote and skip straight away, and those who want to go out of their way to put ‘Yes’ or ‘No’ in there would do so. So I'm not as averse to it, especially if it's just a small fun cosmetic. 
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' On the last point about making Polling more accessible, I still see so many people - especially when we brought up all the Polling changes mention they don't want to go to a Poll Booth to have to vote - they don't have to go out of their way.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I would just like to remind everyone of the Account Management tab. When there is a live Poll, the Account Management tab has a section where you can vote on active Polls from anywhere within Gielinor! You don't have to go to a Poll Booth, you can do it from there!
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' I know there are other options we can look at, but in-game there is an Account Management tab, open it up when there's a Poll live, and you'll be able to see it. There's also a chat message that comes up when there's a currently active and live Poll, so you can get a reminder that there is one there so you're prompted to open the tab and vote from it.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' So spread the word, tell your friends, tell everyone in your clan chat that when there's a Poll live, open the Account Management tab and they'll see the open active Poll button.
* '''Mod Kieren:''' We can also make legitimate improvements to the experience of voting. People miss the notification on login, so potentially add a bigger pop-up of some description to say, ‘Poll live by the way!’, and then it disappears after a few seconds, and even looking at that Account Management UI and making it a bit more intuitive.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' Also, if you ever see a broadcast in-game that talks about a Poll going live, you can right-click and click ‘open’ on that broadcast, which will open the Poll Booth up for you too. We added that functionality last year.
* '''Mod Ayiza:''' We tried to use it as much as possible, but it only goes to people who are online at the time. There are all these little tips and tricks, but there are definitely more improvements that we can make.